## 26. REPLICATION

The preliminary testing should be replicated independently.

Replication should ideally vary:

- model;
- provider;
- prompt structure;
- system instructions;
- task domain;
- context length;
- interaction duration;
- level of autonomy;
- available tools;
- evaluator.

A result that appears only on one model under one prompting configuration should not be treated as general evidence.

The Ox Alpha exercise is therefore a starting point for replication rather than a final validation.

---

## 27. MODEL-DEPENDENCE

Any observed behavioural effect may originate from multiple possible sources:

- the Safe & Sane specification;
- prompt structure;
- model-specific instruction following;
- model alignment;
- system-level policies;
- inference configuration;
- context effects;
- evaluator expectations;
- interaction dynamics.

Therefore causal attribution requires controlled experiments.

The statement:

> "Ox Alpha responded differently after Safe & Sane was introduced"

would be an observation.

The statement:

> "Safe & Sane caused the behavioural improvement"

would be a causal claim requiring experimental support.

This distinction should be maintained throughout the research program.

